Both traces recorded. Summary:

**Subject**: hypre library, specifically the `HYPRE_parcsr_ls` module implementing BoomerAMG. It builds `libHYPRE` (static by default via CMake, `src/CMakeLists.txt`), invoked through user code or hypre's own test drivers (`src/test/new_ij.c`, `src/test/test_ij.c`) and tutorial examples (`src/examples/ex5.c`, `ex11.c`, etc.) that all follow `MPI_Init` → `HYPRE_Initialize()` → build IJ matrix → `HYPRE_BoomerAMGCreate/Setup/Solve`.

**Two variants recorded** (distinguished by GPU enablement, since it changes accelerator/co-location behavior materially):

1. **`cpu-mpi`** (default build): MPI ranks partition rows of the ParCSR matrix; each AMG V-cycle level does nearest-neighbor point-to-point exchange (`hypre_MPI_Isend/Irecv` via `ParCSRCommPkg`, e.g. `par_csr_pmvcomm.c`) for matvec/relaxation halo data, plus `MPI_Allreduce` for norms/dot products in Krylov solvers and setup. OpenMP threading optional (`HYPRE_ENABLE_OPENMP`, `#pragma omp parallel for` box loops). Launched via `mpirun -np N`.

2. **`gpu-cuda`**: same MPI communication skeleton, but built with `HYPRE_ENABLE_CUDA/HIP/SYCL`; matrices/vectors live in device memory by default, AMG setup/solve run on-device, one GPU is explicitly bound per rank (`cudaSetDevice(device_id)` before `HYPRE_Initialize()`), and `HYPRE_ENABLE_GPU_AWARE_MPI` allows direct GPU-to-GPU transfer for the same neighbor-exchange pattern. GPU BoomerAMG restricts to a subset of coarsening/interpolation/smoother options.
